    <!-- Why Is HKT 8 Hours Ahead of GMT? -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>Why Is HKT 8 Hours Ahead of GMT?</h2>
      <p>
        The 8-hour difference between GMT and HKT stems directly from the division of Earth into 24 geographical time zone meridians, spaced at 15-degree longitudinal increments (360° / 24 hours = 15° per hour).
      </p>
      <p>
        Greenwich, London sits at 0° longitude (UTC+0). Hong Kong is centered near 114°10' East longitude. Dividing 120° East (the standard reference longitude for the 8th eastern time zone) by 15° per hour yields:
      </p>
      <p style="text-align: center; font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--accent);">
        120° East / 15° per hour = +8 Hours
      </p>
      <p>
        Because Earth rotates from west to east, regions east of the Prime Meridian see the sun rise earlier. Consequently, Hong Kong enters each new hour exactly 8 hours before Greenwich observatory coordinates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Does GMT to HKT Change the Date? -->
